hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to  another edition of ask Evan today I'm  gonna answer a question from one of our  forum readers who had a question about a  food product that they're trying to sell  so the question here is I have a food  product that does not exist in the  market he uses an existing product when  his main ingredients but then with the  addition of at least four more items  turns into something else that has yet  to be packaged new marketed obviously  the producer the main ingredient would  be the ideal company to sell the idea  recipe to as it can easily use their  production and packaging facilities  complete the new product how would I  approach them and protect my idea but  but still be able to tell them what it  is so thanks for the question a couple  of ideas that come into mind as I read  this the first is usually at least from  my experience the producers of of raw  ingredients or raw supplies are very  rarely interested in creating final  products they know their place in the  food chain and they don't want to expand  it sometimes happens with vertically  integrated companies but it's pretty  rare so chances are the company who  makes this ingredient isn't interested  in making anything beyond their  ingredient and so they may be a  potential partner for you if they think  it's a good idea that may be able to try  to introduce you to some potential  partners or investors or other companies  that they work with who might be able to  help but chances are they wouldn't be  interested themselves it could be wrong  it can happen but for the most part the  companies who produce supplies are  rarely interested in then creating final  products so they're you know just  through the channel you have the  producers you've got the wholesalers you  got the retailers and sometimes a little  bit of crossover but it doesn't happen  that often so I wouldn't think of them  as being the ideal fit as a customer for  this the next thing comes in as to  should I you know protect my idea or not  and this is this is very common this  isn't just for the food business this is  across every entrepreneurial type of  business people are worried that if you  tell people your idea they're gonna run  with it and steal it and turn it into  their own business and you know that can  happen  but your chances of success increase as  you tell people so you have to obviously  be wary of who you tell you don't want  to go up and tell your direct  competition hey here's this great idea  I'm thinking of doing what do you think  they have the easiest ability to copy it  and run with it themselves but if you  don't tell anybody at all you're never  going to hit success right so you have  to tell people about it and this is  something that came up with us commonly  in the venture capital business where  people wouldn't let me look at their  business plan unless we sign the  non-disclosure agreement we never signed  another agreement and it's just part of  the business and you could ask people  for non-disclosure agreements if you're  really concerned about it but chances  are they're not gonna sign it so now  those companies who wouldn't give me  their business plan and they're only  asking for non-disclosure agreements  it's gonna make it a lot harder for them  to get funding for me I'm just trying to  raise money for companies right I'm not  gonna go run and steal somebody's idea I  try to do it myself it's not the  business sign man I'm not interested in  that so by telling me your business plan  idea I see you know I've seen thousands  and thousands of business plans I don't  care if your business plan you know my  only interest here is seen if I can help  you raise money for it I don't want to  run your business it's not what I do  so the same thing with your business  idea you want to go to people who you  think you're potentially help you but  it's not in their business to do what  you're trying to do so you're getting  feedback you're getting advice you're  getting ideas maybe you're getting money  maybe you're getting some partnerships  you'll be getting a team together from  the people you're talking to but you're  not going and talk to your direct  competition so that just a general point  for any entrepreneur who's concerned  about their idea if it's something that  is patentable then you can investigate  going down that route you get a patent  for so that your idea is protected and  any anything like this you could pretend  you get a process patent for so how you  combine things I don't know if it would  really work for recipe but something  worth looking at I guess if it's never  ever been created before but at the end  of the day it's really gonna come down  to execution and picking your partner's  wisely so I would  we I would be cautious over who I tell  the idea to but you have to tell people  the idea if you want to build this up  you have to get the momentum going you  have to get the execution going and you  have to tell people who can potentially  help you with this and not just be  fearful that they're going to take the  idea and run because most people are  locked in to their business and they  they don't want to expand into  completely new ventures that's far  outside what they do so hopefully that  helps those be watching if you liked the  video please give a thumbs up below  thanks want to do more for you and if  you have a question about this video or  question about your own company I'd love  to try to answer your free for I leave  it in the comments below I always read  those and that makes my day when I see  new comments come in so thank you and  I'll see you on the next episode  you
 

